      {
        "id": "def-rpm-h-first-class-constraint-family",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "First-class closure for a regular constraint family",
        "statement": "Let $(P,\\omega)$ be a smooth symplectic manifold with its induced Poisson bracket, and let $\\chi_1,\\ldots,\\chi_r$ be a regular constraint family on a common zero surface $N=\\{z:\\chi_a(z)=0\\}$ as defined by [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]]. The family is **first-class on $N$** when\n\n$$\\{\\chi_a,\\chi_b\\}(z)=0\\qquad\\text{for every }z\\in N\\text{ and all }a,b.$$\n\nEquivalently, each pairwise bracket is weakly zero in the notation $F\\approx0$ of [[def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system]]. The Poisson bracket convention is that of [[thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow]]. This definition is a pointwise closure condition on the stated regular surface; it does not assert multiplier-algorithm termination or global quotient regularity.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system",
          "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-h-regular-constraint-system": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-generated",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-rpm-h-local-hamilton-jacobi",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Local Hamilton-Jacobi solution by invertible characteristics",
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $H\\in C^\\infty(U,\\mathbb R)$ on an open set $U\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*\\mathbb R^n$, with coordinates $(t,q,p)$, and let $S_0\\in C^\\infty(Q_0,\\mathbb R)$ on an open $Q_0\\subset\\mathbb R^n$. For each $a_0\\in Q_0$ there are neighbourhoods of $(0,a_0)$ and a time interval on which the Hamilton characteristic system with $q(0,a)=a$, $p(0,a)=dS_0(a)$ has a smooth solution and $(t,a)\\mapsto(t,q(t,a))$ is a diffeomorphism onto its image after restriction. On that image the function\n\n$$S(t,q(t,a))=S_0(a)+\\int_0^t[p(u,a)\\cdot\\dot q(u,a)-H(u,q(u,a),p(u,a))],du$$\n\nis smooth, has initial value $S_0$, and satisfies $S_t+H(t,q,S_q)=0$. The claim is local to the invertible projection domain; it asserts no continuation after a caustic. $S$ has action units, so $[S]=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $[S_q]=[p]$, and $[S_t]=\\mathrm J$ when $t,q,p,H$ use SI time, length, momentum, and energy units.",
        "deps": [
          "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow",
          "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters",
          "lem-choice-free-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-in-euclidean-space",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-rpm-h-canonical-poisson-and-flow": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-choice-free-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-in-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
